ZTEXT001000014cf6b98abfb0000000
Zero Media 0.14 - original Zero disk images and USB writer

All product implementation in this addition is Zero. Ezero is supported by
the operating system included in the image. No external imaging program,
assembler, hashing library or shell command is executed by these tools.

The .zmedia file is our own ZMEDIA01 container, not an ISO with a new name.
It has a 4096-byte versioned header, exact payload length, logical sector
size, SHA-256 payload checksum, and strictly zero reserved bytes. The limit
is 256 MiB of disk payload. SHA-256 detects corruption; it is not a digital
signature and does not authenticate an unknown publisher.

The payload is a whole bootable disk assembled by our own Zero code. Its
MBR and FAT16 EFI System Partition follow firmware interfaces needed by
existing PCs. The BIOS loaders, EFI loaders, kernels, compiler bundle,
format codec and writer are our implementations. The USB disk uses logical
512-byte sectors. Native 4Kn targets are rejected.

LINUX AND WINDOWS

zero-media info system.zmedia
zero-media verify system.zmedia
zero-drives
zero-flash write system.zmedia DEVICE
zero-flash check system.zmedia DEVICE

On Windows the executable names end in .exe. Both editions are x86-64.
Run the disk programs with administrator privileges. DEVICE is the exact
whole disk path reported by zero-drives, such as /dev/sdb on Linux or
\\.\PhysicalDrive2 on Windows. It is never a drive-letter path or partition.
Visible list numbers begin with one; the PhysicalDrive number is the host
system's actual device name and is not renumbered.

The writer reads the entire source into memory and verifies it before
opening a target. It reports the path, image size and device capacity.
To proceed, type ERASE, one space, and that exact device path, then Enter.
Existing partitions on the selected target are replaced. The writer
rechecks the pinned device identity and capacity after confirmation.
It writes the payload, clears the obsolete backup-GPT area beyond the
image when present, flushes, and compares every written byte on readback.
An interrupted write is reported as incomplete; this is not an atomic
disk replacement. Remaining unused disk space is not securely erased.

Only removable USB whole disks with a readable stable serial identity
are eligible. Internal, read-only, native-4Kn, busy and unidentified disks
are rejected. Linux volumes must be unmounted and swap disabled. Windows
volumes must have no drive-letter or directory mount paths; all associated
volumes are enumerated and locked, and the Windows system disk is excluded.
The tools do not silently unmount a filesystem. A failed safety check may
reject an otherwise usable USB device. Details are in HOST-BACKEND.ztext.

NONDESTRUCTIVE FILE TEST

zero-flash inspect system.zmedia
zero-flash test system.zmedia NEW_FILE

test exclusively creates a new regular file, writes the disk payload,
flushes it and checks every byte. It cannot replace an existing file or
open a block device. This does not test the physical USB hardware path.

OWN CODEC

zero-media make DISK_PAYLOAD NEW.zmedia
zero-media extract IMAGE.zmedia NEW_DISK_PAYLOAD
zero-media-check

The make/extract output must not already exist. Invalid input never creates
an output. A new output that fails during writing is retained for diagnosis;
the program never deletes a path after another process could replace it.
extract is for interoperability with VM disk tools, not a requirement for
zero-flash. The .zmedia container itself is not a CD-ROM image and should
not be attached as an ISO to VirtualBox.

ZERO FIRMWARE EDITION

The Zero edition runs before the kernel using UEFI Block I/O. It is a
separate original Zero program, not an in-kernel USB driver. The current
Zero kernel still has ATA storage only. Use the Zero UEFI boot menu to
launch Flash Writer. BIOS can boot the installed system, but the firmware
writer requires UEFI. Secure Boot signing is not provided.

Place a file named system.zmedia at the root of a separate filesystem
that the firmware can read, normally a FAT volume. The writer identifies
available image sources and removable USB destinations. The utility's
boot disk and the selected source disk are protected from selection as
a target. The 16 MiB boot ESP is too small to hold this 18 MiB image;
keep the source image on a separate readable volume. The image is fully
checked before confirmation, writing, flushing and full readback.

BUILDING WITH OUR TOOLS

zero-build PROJECT NEW_BUILD_DIRECTORY
zero-disk NEW_BUILD_DIRECTORY disk-bootstrap.bin disk-bios.bin NEW_DISK
zero-media make NEW_DISK NEW.zmedia

For zero-disk, use the actual disk-bootstrap.bin and disk-bios.bin paths
inside the new build directory. zero-build compiles all platform editions
using our own compiler. Canonical composed .zero sources are included.
media/compose.zero reconstructs flash, drives and native backend sources;
media/compose-codec.zero reconstructs the codec and its checks;
media/firmware-compose.zero reconstructs the firmware sources.

The verification record distinguishes real emulated USB boot/write tests,
native Linux file tests, Windows/Wine tests, and untested physical devices.
Physical USB drives and native Windows hardware are not certified by the
emulator tests. See VERIFICATION.ztext for the exact completed checks.
